<main id="main">
  <section class="hero ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>What AI data actually sells for.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Public deals, published rates and the ranges buyers advertise &mdash; each with a source and a date, including the figures that undercut the pitch. Checked 10 November 2026.</p>
      <p><a class="btn" href="contact.html">Tell us about your data</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="kicker">Read this first</p>
        <h2>Almost every number you have seen is a range, not a receipt.</h2>
        <p>Three things are true of this market at once, and most broker sites mention only the first.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="icon-tile" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-info"/></svg></span>
          <h3>The headline ranges are marketing</h3>
          <p>When a buyer advertises &ldquo;up to $5M&rdquo;, that is the top of a band, not a typical result. The same band is repeated across the category because everyone cites the same few buyers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="icon-tile" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-doc"/></svg></span>
          <h3>Few deals disclose an amount</h3>
          <p>Of 130 disclosed licensing deals tracked publicly, only about 23 carry a dollar figure. The rest confirm a deal happened and say nothing about price.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="icon-tile" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-scales"/></svg></span>
          <h3>A price is a bundle</h3>
          <p>Rights, scope, term, exclusivity and payment timing move a number more than the data type does. Two archives of the same size can differ by an order of magnitude.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="kicker">The public record</p>
        <h2>Deals with a published amount.</h2>
        <p>Reported figures, not agreements we have seen. Where a tracker describes a range or an estimate, it is marked as one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap reveal">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th>Deal</th><th>Figure</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Spirit Airlines&rsquo; archive to Google</td><td>$10M, sold through the airline&rsquo;s bankruptcy</td><td>Rightmark deal tracker</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reddit&rsquo;s content to Google</td><td>About $60M a year; $203M over two to three years</td><td>AI Training Data Broker</td></tr>
            <tr><td>News Corp to OpenAI</td><td>Reported at more than $250M over five years</td><td>AI Training Data Broker</td></tr>
            <tr><td>HarperCollins titles to Microsoft</td><td>$5,000 a title for three years</td><td>Rightmark deal tracker</td></tr>
            <tr><td>De-identified patient records</td><td>About $100 a record</td><td>Rightmark deal tracker</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="muted reveal">These are large publishers, a bankrupt airline and a medical dataset. None is a mid-market operating company, which is the market we work in, and none of them tells you what your records are worth.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="kicker">Unit rates</p>
        <h2>What buyers pay per unit, where a rate is published.</h2>
        <p>Content rates, not company records. They show how buyers price a unit, and why an archive is worth more than a pile of files.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap reveal">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Median reported value</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>News article</td><td>$0.46</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Image</td><td>$1.12</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Minute of video</td><td>$2.65</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hour of transcribed audio</td><td>$58</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Book</td><td>$4,300</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="muted reveal">Medians reported by AI Industry Reviews for 2026. A support archive is not priced by the ticket, because the value sits in the sequence: the problem, the diagnosis, the fix and the outcome.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="kicker">The range everyone quotes</p>
        <h2>$100K to $5M. It is a ceiling, not a forecast.</h2>
        <p>The buyer program we introduce companies to publishes $100K&ndash;$5M for an operating archive. micro1 advertises $200K&ndash;$2M+. Both are ranges a buyer publishes before it has seen your data. The figure that matters arrives after a rights review and a scope, in writing, and it is the one you can refuse.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="link-row reveal"><a href="what-affects-a-deal.html">What moves the number</a> <a href="ai-buyers.html">Questions to ask any buyer</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="kicker">Our commitment</p>
        <h2>What we will publish when we have it.</h2>
        <p>This page is other people&rsquo;s numbers, labelled as such. We do not have our own yet, and inventing them would make us identical to every site above. When we have closed deals we will publish the count, the median, the days from first call to payment, and the ones that fell through.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="reveal">Until then, treat every figure on this page as evidence about the market, not as a quote for your company. The only number that binds anyone is the one in an agreement you have read.</p>
      <p class="link-row reveal"><a href="trust.html">How we handle your data</a> <a href="faq.html">All questions</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

